cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  falsifyloop: requirement falsification for autonomous control loops, with
  sample-efficiency curves measured against uniform random
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-08
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A requirement falsification package for simulated control loops. It provides a
  small, fully documented requirement language -- bounded always and eventually,
  bounds on signals and on their first differences, conjunction and disjunction
  -- whose robustness semantics is negative exactly when the requirement is
  violated. That equivalence is not asserted: it is checked against an
  independently implemented Boolean semantics over 44694 exhaustively enumerated
  formula-trace pairs with zero disagreements, including 32070 samples at
  exactly zero robustness and 16848 at infinite robustness, and property-tested
  with Hypothesis. The language omits negation deliberately, so that every
  formula is in negation normal form and the sign agreement is exact at the
  boundary rather than within a tolerance band. Eight seeded benchmark instances
  share one declared six-dimensional search box over a synthetic single-axis
  attitude loop with a rate-limited actuator, and span measured uniform-violation
  probabilities from 0.321933 down to 0.001267 with exact Clopper-Pearson
  intervals. Five search strategies run over the same instances, the same budget
  and the same seeds: uniform random, Latin hypercube, simulated annealing,
  cross-entropy, and a random-forest surrogate-guided search with an ensemble
  dispersion output. The deliverable is the sample-efficiency curve -- the
  probability of having found a violation against simulation count, with
  pointwise bootstrap bands -- reported per instance before any aggregate.
  Uniform random is the baseline and several published results favour it:
  Latin hypercube and cross-entropy never beat it on any of the eight instances,
  cross-entropy found nothing in thirty runs on the hardest one, and simulated
  annealing loses resolvably on every easy-to-moderate instance. The
  surrogate-guided search wins on aggregate but only where the baseline is
  already struggling, and fourteen of thirty-two per-instance comparisons are
  statistically undecided at thirty seeds, which is reported rather than hidden.
  The surrogate's uncertainty output is measured and found not to be calibrated,
  and is used only as a ranking signal. Falsification is one-sided: finding no
  violation is not evidence of correctness, and the command-line tool cannot
  print anything that reads as a pass. Validation level 3, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - falsification
  - signal-temporal-logic
  - robustness-semantics
  - sample-efficiency
  - surrogate-optimisation
  - random-forest
  - requirement-monitoring
  - aerospace-assurance
